  "overview": "The Pro Mini strips the Arduino down to just the chip, crystal, regulator, and pin headers — no onboard USB, no bootloader-friendly reset button, nothing that isn't strictly necessary. That makes it the smallest, cheapest way to get a 'real' Arduino into a permanent project, at the cost of needing an external USB-to-serial adapter (an 'FTDI' programmer) every time you want to upload code. It comes in 5V/16MHz and 3.3V/8MHz variants — mixing them up is the single most common Pro Mini mistake, since running 5V logic into a 3.3V board damages it.",
  "simpleSummary": "A stripped-down Arduino with no USB port, made to be built permanently into a project.",
